{{History of Balisca}}

The '''1919 Balisca coup d'état attempt''' was a failed [[W:Coup d'état|coup attempt]] in [[Balisca]] taking place from 13 to 20 June 1919, conducted by the [[W:Comintern|Comintern]], and staged by the [[Communist Party of Balisca]] and [[W:Bolsheviks|Bolsheviks]] who in most part had infiltrated the party from [[W:Soviet Russia|Soviet Russia]]. The uprising was intended to prevent the consolidation of the [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchist]] influence in the [[1919 Confederal Convention]] during the [[Baliscan Revolution]], and to establish a centralized socialist republic aligned with the Bolshevik model. The coup failed due to lack of popular support, internal fragmentation, and decisive resistance by [[Autonomous Militia|Autonomous Militias]] loyal to the democratic revolutionary movement.

== Background and events ==
In the autumn of 1919 elections were held to select the delegates that would attend that year's Confederal Convention. Tensions were running high as the [[W:Popular front|popular front]] between the socialists, anarchists and communists began to fray in the face of confederal elections. Despite the Communist Party's ambitions, the anarchists were able to secure a majority of rural delegations and a slim plurality of urban delegations, in part due to their influence in industrial syndicates, agricultural collectives, and local councils.

The strong showing of the anarchists in urban centers reflected deeper political and structural shifts that were taking place before-and strengthened during-the Revolution. In many major cities, [[W:Anarcho-syndicalism|anarcho-syndicalist]] unions had established themselves as the dominant force in key sectors such as transport''',''' dockyards''',''' printing''',''' and metallurgy, in which they favored decentralized self-management over party-led control. These unions, along with commune assemblies and [[W:Open cabildo|open cabildos]], had taken on a larger role in urban governance during the [[Cosmocazo|pre-revolutionary period]], often supplanting corrupt and ineffective local administrations. Their success was also buoyed by their mutual aid infrastructure—free schools, food distribution networks, medical brigades, and printing cooperatives—which had provided vital support during the social upheaval. In contrast, the Communist Party was increasingly viewed as marred by internal factionalism and guided by foreign (Bolshevik) influence.

Fearing marginalization and the opposing the looming codification of libertarian socialist principles into the Basic Laws, the Communist Party’s Leninist faction began organizing armed militias, drawn largely from urban partisan remnants and a small number of disaffected factory guards. On 13 June these militias launched a coordinated uprising in Castejón, aiming to dissolve the Convention by force, seize key government infrastructure, and ultimately declare a [[W:Marxist–Leninist state|Marxist–Leninist state]]. In response, the Autonomous Militias, already mobilized as a result of the events of the [[Tragic Week]], acted rapidly and decisively to confront the Communist Party's Red Guards (militias).

In a brief but intense series of urban confrontations, now known as the [[May Days]], the communists declared a [[Provisional Central Committee]] as a rival governing authority and attempted to arrest or assassinate anarchist delegates. Fighting persisted for a week, but the communists—lacking broad working-class support and plagued by internal factional disputes—were largely thwarted in their attempt within the first few days. By the end of June the last holdouts had surrendered or went into self-imposed exile to [[W:Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic|Soviet Russia]]. The majority of rank-and-file rebels were spared execution; only senior party leaders and conspirators were tried for sedition or conspiracy to sabotage the revolutionary process.

